INT. HOSPITAL PARKING GARAGE - LEVEL 3 - NIGHT

Concrete. Humming lights. A lone sedan with its hazards blinking. Rain beats on the ramp.

A WOMAN, 50s, in scrubs and a trench coat, sits on the bumper, face in her hands.

A MAN, 30s, in a hospital badge lanyard, approaches from the stairwell with two coffees.

MAN
Dr. Linden?

WOMAN
Don't.

MAN
I'm Peter. From the ER. You asked me to come.

She lifts her head. Her mascara has run.

WOMAN
I didn't ask you anything.

PETER
You did. Eight minutes ago. Text message.

WOMAN
I don't remember.

He sets a coffee on the hood.

PETER
You lost the boy in bay six.

DR. LINDEN
I lost the boy in bay six.

PETER ^
We all did.

She takes the coffee. Holds it without drinking.

DR. LINDEN
He was nine.

PETER
I know.

DR. LINDEN
He had a dog named Rocket.

PETER
Dr. Linden...

DR. LINDEN
He told me. While I was putting in the line.

Peter sits beside her. Their shoulders touch. Rain drums on the roof.

PETER
Come back inside. I'll cover your shift.

DR. LINDEN
I can't.

PETER
You can.

She nods. Doesn't move.
